    "case_id": "354",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 60s developed asthenia, anorexia, and cough. Six days later, he was hospitalized with diffuse bilateral pneumonia.\n\nNumerous 3- to 6-mm pseudovesicular papules with superficial crusting were noted on the trunk. The papules were asymptomatic. Within 48 hours the papules evolved to an extensive, but not confluent, purpuric rash.\n\n{'Laboratory Tests': '- Nasopharyngeal swab using real-time reverse transcriptase–polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR)- Diagnostic testing on superficial skin sample for herpes simplex, varicella zoster, and SARS-CoV-2 viruses (all negative)- Immunohistochemical testing for herpes simplex and varicella zoster viruses (negative)- RT-PCR testing of fixed skin tissue for SARS-CoV-2 (negative)', 'Pathology': 'Histologic analysis showed:- Extensive epidermal necrosis with acantholysis- Large multinucleated keratinocytes with ballooning degeneration- Dense perivascular lymphohistiocytic infiltrate with extravasated erythrocytes and eosinophils in superficial dermis- Early-stage vasculitic alterations with endothelial swelling of dermal vessels without fibrinoid necrosis or thrombosis', 'Images': {'Figure 1': {'Title': 'Papulovesicular Eruption', 'Description': 'Multiple papules with a pseudovesicular aspect and superficial crust are seen on the trunk'}, 'Figure 2': {'Title': 'Histologic Analysis of the Biopsy Specimen', 'Description': 'Shows area of extensive epidermal necrosis with acantholysis and swelling of keratinocytes due to intracellular edema. Includes views of large multinucleated keratinocytes with ballooning degeneration and perivascular lymphohistiocytic infiltrate'}}}",
